/*
# Toolbar

A row of commands that is one Tab stop — a text editor's Bold, Italic and Underline, a
drawing app's tools. Tab lands on its first command (or the one last used), the arrows move
along the row, Home and End go to its ends, and the next Tab leaves it, so a long row of
commands costs one stop. It sits on the same rounded track a `Segmented` does, its
`ToolButton`s quiet until hovered:

```declare
use [ PlusIcon, MinusIcon ]

App [ width = 360, height = 100, bold: boolean = false, italic: boolean = false,
    Toolbar [ x = 20, y = 20, accessibilityLabel = "Formatting",
        ToolButton [ label = "B", toggle = true, on = { app.bold }, input(v: boolean) { app.bold = v },
            accessibilityLabel = "Bold" ],
        ToolButton [ label = "I", toggle = true, on = { app.italic }, input(v: boolean) { app.italic = v },
            accessibilityLabel = "Italic" ],
        ToolbarDivider [ ],
        ToolButton [ icon = "PlusIcon", label = "Add" ],
        ToolButton [ icon = "MinusIcon", label = "Remove" ]
        ]
    ]
```

Its children are yours: `ToolButton`s, a `ToolbarDivider` between groups, or any control —
a `Segmented`, a `Stepper` — which the arrows reach like the rest. To a screen reader it is a
toolbar, named at its use site.

## accessibilityLabel
The toolbar's name — what its commands are for ("Formatting"). Set it at the use site.

## focusScope
`stop`: the toolbar is one entry in the Tab order; the arrows move among its commands.

## height
The theme's `segmentedHeight`, as a `Segmented` is.

## cornerRadius
The theme's `segmentedCornerRadius`.

## fill
The track: the theme's `trackFill`, else `control`.

## padding
2 all round, so a command's hover sits inside the track as a segment's does.
*/

include [ "control.declare", "icons/icon.declare", "icons/core.declare" ]

class Toolbar [
    accessibilityRole = toolbar,
    accessibilityLabel = "",
    focusScope = stop,
    height = { provided("theme").segmentedHeight },
    cornerRadius = { provided("theme").segmentedCornerRadius },
    fill = { provided("theme").trackFill ?? provided("theme").control },
    padding = 2,
    layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = x, spacing = 2, align = center ]
    ]


/*
# ToolButton

One command in a `Toolbar`: a label or an icon, quiet until hovered. A `toggle` holds a
state — Bold on or off — and shows it with the raised pill a `Segmented` shows its choice
with.

```declare
App [ width = 300, height = 80, wrap: boolean = true,
    Toolbar [ x = 20, y = 20, accessibilityLabel = "View",
        ToolButton [ label = "Wrap", toggle = true, on = { app.wrap }, input(v: boolean) { app.wrap = v } ],
        ToolButton [ label = "Zoom in" ]
        ]
    ]
```

## label
The command's text. With an `icon`, it is the button's name and tooltip instead of its text.

## icon
An icon's class name (`"PlusIcon"`), shown in place of the label. An icon named by a string
is built by name, so the program keeps it with `use [ PlusIcon ]`.

## toggle
Whether the button holds a state, on or off. A toggle is a pressed or unpressed button to a
screen reader.

## on
A toggle's state. Constrain it to your own state and override `input` to own the
destination, or leave it alone and read it back.

## input()
**Where a toggle's change goes**, receiving the new `on`. The default writes `on`.

## onClick
What the command does — the handler you give it. A toggle's own flips `on`; a toggle's
change is had through `input`, so give a toggle no `onClick`.

## press()
Runs `onClick`, so Space and Enter do what a click does.

## accessibilityPressed
A toggle's `on` — a toggle is a button that is pressed or not; a plain command reports none.

## accessibilityLabel
The `label`, when the button shows an icon.

## tooltipLabel
The `label`, when the button shows an icon.

## width
The label or icon and 10 either side, and never narrower than it is tall.

## height
As tall as the toolbar's inside.

## cornerRadius
2 inside the toolbar's.

## fill
The pill when a toggle is on (the theme's `segmentedSelectedFill`, else `surface`); the
control states when hovered or pressed; else none.

## stroke
A hairline in the theme's `line` around a toggle that is on, as around a segment's pill.

## opacity
Disabled dims the button, to the theme's `disabledOpacity`.

## _stateFill
**Internal.** The ground for the pressed or hovered state, or none at rest, that `fill` uses when not `on`.
*/

class ToolButton extends Control [
    label: string = "",
    icon: string = "",
    toggle: boolean = false,
    on: boolean = false,
    input(v: boolean) { on = v },
    onClick() { if (toggle) input(!on) },
    press() { onClick() },

    accessibilityRole = button,
    accessibilityPressed = { toggle ? on : null },
    accessibilityLabel = { icon != "" ? label : "" },
    tooltipLabel = { icon != "" ? label : "" },

    height = { (parent as View).height - 4 },
    width = { Math.max(this.height, (icon != "" ? this.ico.width : this.t.width) + 20) },
    cornerRadius = { Math.max(0, provided("theme").segmentedCornerRadius - 2) },
    _stateFill: Color? = { pressed ? provided("theme").controlPressed : hovered ? provided("theme").controlHovered : null },
    fill = { toggle && on ? (provided("theme").segmentedSelectedFill ?? provided("theme").surface) : this._stateFill },
    stroke = { toggle && on ? stroke(1, provided("theme").line) : null },
    opacity = { disabled ? provided("theme").disabledOpacity : 1 },

    t: TextLabel [ x = center, wrap = false, visible = { classroot.icon == "" },
        fontSize = { provided("theme").segmentedFontSize }, fontWeight = medium,
        textColor = { classroot.on || classroot.hovered || !classroot.toggle ? provided("theme").text : provided("theme").textMuted },
        text = { classroot.label } ],
    ico: IconHost [ x = center, y = center, kind = { classroot.icon }, ink = { provided("theme").text } ]
    ]


/*
# ToolbarDivider

A hairline between groups of commands in a `Toolbar`. To a screen reader, a separator.

```declare
App [ width = 300, height = 80,
    Toolbar [ x = 20, y = 20, accessibilityLabel = "Edit",
        ToolButton [ label = "Cut" ], ToolButton [ label = "Copy" ],
        ToolbarDivider [ ],
        ToolButton [ label = "Undo" ]
        ]
    ]
```

## width
1, with the toolbar's spacing either side.

## height
Half the toolbar's height.

## fill
The theme's `line`.
*/

class ToolbarDivider [
    accessibilityRole = separator,
    width = 1,
    height = { (parent as View).height / 2 },
    fill = { provided("theme").line }
    ]
